Original commit body from eee7c62:

Previously, the representativeness metric for Additional Files summed
per-file normalized scores (in-degree/filesize), which didn't accurately
represent "how much symbol connectivity is covered." If README.md and
CONTRIBUTING.md both mentioned `foo`, we were double-counting foo's
contribution.

Now we:
1. Track which symbols are mentioned in each file (symbols_per_file)
2. When calculating representativeness, collect unique symbols across
   all selected files
3. Sum in-degrees only for those unique symbols (no double-counting)

This is analogous to how Source Files representativeness counts each
symbol definition only once, regardless of how many times it appears.

Also sums in-degrees for symbols with the same name (e.g., foo in
module1.py and foo in module2.py) since a doc mentioning "foo"
documents all symbols with that name.

Changes:
- Add CentralityResult dataclass with normalized_scores, symbols_per_file,
  and name_to_in_degree fields
- Update compute_symbol_mention_centrality_batch to return CentralityResult
- Update _format_additional_files to use de-duplicated in-degree sum
- Fix cli.py run_behavior_map to use new return type
